COMMENT: Exercise 1. Lower-third slides in from left over 400 ms while fading up,
over a Ken Burns photo. Model used: flat properties; a transform property is a
number OR a keyframe list; keyframe times RELATIVE to element start; record shape
{"t":..,"v":..,"ease":..}. Frame 1080x1920.

{"id":"kb-photo","type":"image","start":5000,"end":20000,"source":"images/05.png","x":0,"y":0,"origin":"top-left","width":1080,"height":1300,"fit":"cover","crop_align":"top","scale":[{"t":0,"v":[1.0,1.0]},{"t":15000,"v":[1.08,1.08]}]}

{"id":"lower-third","type":"text","start":5000,"end":9000,"font":"brand","size":55,"color":"#FFF8E8","origin":"bottom-left","align":"start","line_height":1.1,"x":[{"t":0,"v":-420},{"t":400,"v":80,"ease":"out"}],"y":1700,"opacity":[{"t":0,"v":0.0},{"t":400,"v":1.0,"ease":"out"}],"runs":[{"text":"Jane Doe\nField Producer"}]}

NOTES WHILE WRITING:
- Two animated properties on one element = two keyframe lists that must agree on
  400. Nothing ties them; if I retime the slide and forget the fade they desync
  silently. Lived cost, accepted: an "animation block" would be nesting by another name.
- I wanted x to start "one card-width off screen". -420 is a guess at the card's
  measured width; the real number needs `measure`. Same unknowable-number class as
  FINDINGS C1/C2.
- Under ABSOLUTE keyframe times these two lists would read [{"t":5000,...},{"t":5400,...}]
  and the Ken Burns list [{"t":5000,"v":1.0},{"t":20000,"v":1.08}] — every list retypes
  the element's own start. Relative: the slide-in is the same four bytes on any element.
